C++20 std::iterator_traits<__normal_iterator<...>>::iterator_category resolution.

KNOWNBUG: the C++20 std::__iterator_traits has three partial specializations
sharing the template-argument pattern <_Iterator, void>, distinguished only by
their requires-clauses (concept constraints __iter_with_nested_types /
__iter_without_nested_types && __cpp17_*_iterator).  The front end does not yet
select among same-pattern partial specializations by their associated
constraints ([temp.class.spec.match]/1-2 with [temp.constr.order]), so
iterator_traits<__normal_iterator<...>>::iterator_category does not resolve to
the iterator's nested random_access_iterator_tag.

Note: iterator_traits<int*>::iterator_category and
std::vector<int>::iterator::iterator_category each resolve correctly in
isolation; only the primary iterator_traits<It> (It a class) path -- which goes
through the constrained __iterator_traits specialization -- fails.

This blocks the std::__find_if tag dispatch (and hence std::find / std::erase_if
on real containers), which deduces the 4-argument overload from
std::__iterator_category(first).

Reclassify CORE once constrained partial specialization selection is supported.
